// An element read out of a list that is meant to own its elements
// (docs/MEMORY.md 3.2). `xs[i]` is another name for the element, so when its
// value goes somewhere that keeps it -- ctx 1: a store, a return, a callee that
// keeps the argument -- the list no longer owns that element alone, and
// freeing it with the list would leave the keeper dangling. Flag bit 4. A read
// that only looks (ctx 0: an operand, a length, an index of the element) sets
// nothing, and neither does a read of something with nothing to free (an int,
// a float): only a string, a list or a struct can be freed under its holder.
//
// A struct is freed by its type, all the way down (3.5b), so for a type that
// carries one the read need not be of an element: any path `t.a[i].b` from the
// name that reaches a string, a list or a struct, and goes into a position that
// keeps it or whose callee may break the ownership of what it is handed (ctx
// bit 8), sets bit 32.
esc_elem(int e, string nm, int ctx) {
  int r = 0;
  if(esc_chain(e, nm) == 1 && esc_heap_ty(type_of(e)) == 1) {
    r = esc_elem2(e, ctx);
  }
} return int r;
